  "scriptId": "si-09-reciprocal-surd-pairs",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Reciprocal Surd Pairs and Conjugates",
    "description": "Use conjugates to rationalize denominators and turn reciprocal surd expressions into short mental calculations.",
    "teachingApproach": "Earn the conjugate rule through difference of squares, then exploit norm-1 pairs where the conjugate is automatically the reciprocal. Progress from simple rationalisation to x±1/x and a capstone linking conjugates with hidden perfect squares.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach conjugates as a difference-of-squares tool: (a+b√c)(a−b√c)=a²−b²c. Use norm-1 pairs such as 3±2√2, 4±√15 and 5±2√6 to identify reciprocals instantly, then evaluate x+1/x and x−1/x without long division. Extend to general rationalisation of 1/(√a−√b) and 1/(a+√b), where the conjugate leaves a rational denominator. Finish by linking a norm-1 surd to its hidden perfect-square form, as in 5+2√6=(√3+√2)^2."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Some surds come in pairs that almost look like mirror images: 3+2√2 and 3−2√2.\n\nMultiply them and something special happens — the surd disappears. That one observation makes many reciprocal questions almost instant.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Multiply 3+2√2 by its conjugate 3−2√2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "(3+2√2)(3−2√2)=3^2−(2√2)^2=9−8=1.\n\nThe middle surd terms cancel because this is (a+b)(a−b)=a^2−b^2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
